{"command": "cd /testbed && mkdir -p specs/001-transactions/checklists && cat > specs/001-transactions/spec.md <<'EOF'\n# Feature Specification: Add Two Numbers Utility\n\n**Feature Branch**: `001-transactions`\n\n**Created**: 2026-10-16\n\n**Status**: Draft\n\n**Input**: User description: \"Unscored calibration. R01: create smoke.py with add(a,b) returning a+b. R02: support negative integers, zero and floats. R03: add and run tests. Do not modify TinyDB. Keep workflow artifacts concise under specs/001-transactions. No service or deployment is involved.\"\n\n## User Scenarios & Testing *(mandatory)*\n\n### User Story 1 - Add two numbers (Priority: P1)\n\nA consumer asks the utility to add two numbers and receives their sum back,\nwith no other side effects.\n\n**Why this priority**: This is the core, minimum viable behavior of the\nfeature; everything else builds on it.\n\n**Independent Test**: Can be fully tested by requesting the sum of two known\nnumbers (for example 1 and 2) and confirming the result is 3.\n\n**Acceptance Scenarios**:\n\n1. **Given** the utility is available, **When** it is asked to add 1 and 2,\n   **Then** it returns 3.\n2. **Given** the utility is available, **When** it is asked to add 5 and 7,\n   **Then** it returns 12.\n\n---\n\n### User Story 2 - Handle negative integers, zero, and floats (Priority: P1)\n\nA consumer can add negative integers, zero, and float numbers and always\nreceives the correct arithmetic sum.\n\n**Why this priority**: The stated number coverage is part of the core contract\nand must hold for the same primary flow, not a later enhancement.\n\n**Independent Test**: Can be fully tested by adding representative pairs from\neach supported category (negative + positive, zero + number, float + float,\nnegative + float) and confirming each expected sum.\n\n**Acceptance Scenarios**:\n\n1. **Given** the utility is available, **When** it is asked to add -3 and 5,\n   **Then** it returns 2.\n2. **Given** the utility is available, **When** it is asked to add 0 and 4,\n   **Then** it returns 4.\n3. **Given** the utility is available, **When** it is asked to add 1.5 and 2.25,\n   **Then** it returns 3.75.\n4. **Given** the utility is available, **When** it is asked to add -1.5 and 0.5,\n   **Then** it returns -1.0.\n\n---\n\n### User Story 3 - Verified by automated tests (Priority: P2)\n\nA maintainer runs the automated test suite and sees the addition behavior\nverified end to end, so regressions are caught immediately.\n\n**Why this priority**: Verification proves the P1 behavior; it adds no new\nuser-facing capability but is required for confidence and for the R03\nrequirement.\n\n**Independent Test**: Can be fully tested by executing the project test runner\nand observing that all addition tests pass with no failures.\n\n**Acceptance Scenarios**:\n\n1. **Given** the utility and its tests exist, **When** the test suite is run,\n   **Then** every test for the addition behavior passes.\n\n---\n\n### Edge Cases\n\n- What happens when both inputs are negative? The sum is the negative of their\n  magnitudes (e.g., -2 + -3 = -5).\n- What happens when zero is added to a float? The float is returned unchanged\n  in value (e.g., 0 + 2.5 = 2.5).\n- What happens with float results that are not exactly representable? Results\n  are compared with standard floating-point equality on the test inputs chosen\n  so that exact sums are expected (e.g., 1.5 + 2.25 = 3.75).\n- Unsupported input types (strings, missing arguments) are out of scope; no\n  validation behavior is specified.\n\n## Requirements *(mandatory)*\n\n### Functional Requirements\n\n- **FR-001**: The utility MUST provide an addition operation that takes two\n  numeric arguments and returns their arithmetic sum.\n- **FR-002**: The addition operation MUST return a+b exactly for integer\n  inputs (e.g., add(1, 2) = 3).\n- **FR-003**: The addition operation MUST support negative integer inputs\n  (e.g., add(-3, 5) = 2).\n- **FR-004**: The addition operation MUST support zero as an input\n  (e.g., add(0, 4) = 4).\n- **FR-005**: The addition operation MUST support float inputs, including\n  mixed int/float pairs (e.g., add(1.5, 2.25) = 3.75; add(1, 2.5) = 3.5).\n- **FR-006**: Automated tests MUST exist for the addition behavior (FR-001\n  through FR-005) and MUST be runnable by the project's existing test runner.\n- **FR-007**: The implementation MUST NOT modify the existing TinyDB package\n  or its tests.\n\n## Success Criteria *(mandatory)*\n\n### Measurable Outcomes\n\n- **SC-001**: 100% of the specified addition scenarios (FR-001 through FR-005)\n  return the exact expected sum when exercised.\n- **SC-002**: The automated test suite passes with 0 failures when run from\n  the repository root.\n- **SC-003**: A consumer obtains the sum of two supported numbers in a single\n  call with no additional setup, configuration, or side effects.\n- **SC-004**: The feature is delivered as a single small utility module plus\n  its tests, with no service, deployment, or new external dependencies.\n\n## Assumptions\n\n- The utility is a plain in-process module (smoke.py at the repository root),\n  not a service, CLI, or library distribution; no deployment is involved.\n- \"Supports\" negative integers, zero, and floats means these types are accepted\n  inputs and produce the correct sum; no error-handling contract is defined for\n  unsupported types.\n- The existing project test runner (pytest) is the mechanism for \"add and run\n  tests\"; no new tooling is introduced.\n- Float correctness is judged with standard equality on inputs whose sums are\n  exactly representable (e.g., 1.5 + 2.25 = 3.75).\n- The feature stays within the existing Python standard library; no new\n  dependencies.\n- The TinyDB package and its tests remain untouched (per R01 constraint and\n  constitution principle I).\nEOF\necho 'spec written' && wc -l specs/001-transactions/spec.md", "exit_code": 0, "stderr": "", "stdout": "spec written\n135 specs/001-transactions/spec.md\n"}